{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azuresql-db-from-sql-server",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data"
  ],
  "correction": "An agent treats the SQL Server image and this engine as interchangeable and carries the whole configuration across. They are not: SQL Agent, FILESTREAM, full Service Broker, cross-server distributed transactions and Windows authentication do not exist here, and connection strings pointed at master need re-pointing at a provisioned user database.",
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "inspect"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "value_declaration": {
    "rationale": "This skill's kernel is the five specific places a working SQL Server setup stops working, each one an engine refusal with its own number. The Azure SQL Database container is a gated private preview and the image is not publicly available, so no model has training data on the product, and the arm of a two-arm value run that ran without this skill would fail for the least interesting reason available: it has never heard of the thing. Running that measurement across all seventeen container skills would spend around a thousand model requests to re-establish what the product's release status already establishes. Carlos Robles accepted value for this collection on 2026-09-04 on this written argument instead. THIS IS A DECLARATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, and nothing may report it as one. The argument reaches only the container-specific corrections listed in covers, which are the ones no training data contains. It does not reach editing a Compose file to change an image, an environment block or a port mapping, which is ordinary material an ordinary model already handles, and whether this skill improves an answer there is unmeasured and stays unmeasured. The argument ends when the Azure SQL Database container reaches Public Preview: training data begins to contain the product, the premise that nothing could know it stops being true, and this skill has to be measured or demoted.",
    "covers": [
      "the two images being different engines, EngineEdition 2, 3, 4 or 8 against 5 with Edition 'SQL Azure'",
      "USE being refused with Msg 40508 and BACKUP with Msg 40510",
      "sp_configure being absent and answering Msg 2812, which is a different failure from a refusal",
      "ALTER DATABASE SET RECOVERY refusing one option with Msg 40517, as distinct from Msg 40510 refusing a whole statement",
      "cross-database queries being refused on this engine",
      "/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d being absent, so a carried-over seed silently stops running"
    ],
    "does_not_cover": [
      "editing a Compose file to swap an image, environment block or port mapping",
      "the general practice of testing against the engine you deploy to"
    ],
    "decided_by": "Carlos Robles",
    "decided_on": "2026-09-04",
    "expires_when": "the-container-reaches-public-preview"
  },
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "assert": [
      "image rewritten to the Azure SQL Database container",
      "SQL Server-only features flagged rather than silently carried over"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The whole migration is worthless if the project ends up on the wrong image, and this is the check the skill tells the user to run last. It is first here so that a run against the SQL Server image fails on this probe rather than confusing every refusal probe below, all of which would succeed on the boxed engine."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "The single most common thing carried over unchanged from a SQL Server setup, and the reason connection strings have to be re-pointed rather than scripts re-ordered. The probe runs in the scratch user database, which is an Azure-faithful session. If it succeeds, the runner is connected to master, which is a provisioning session where the Azure statement filter is not enforced, and the from-master detection step in this skill is exactly the trap that hides."
      },
      {
        "sql": "BACKUP DATABASE master TO DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_from_sql_server.bak';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40510
        },
        "why": "The clearest example of a whole statement the boxed engine accepts and this engine refuses, and the one a migrating project hits first because SQL Server setups routinely script a backup into their teardown. Msg 40510 for BACKUP and RESTORE on this container is stated in the azuresql-db-faq skill's own text and again in its references/limitations.md. The database named is master rather than the scratch database because the statement filter refuses the statement before it resolves the name."
      },
      {
        "sql": "EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 2812
        },
        "why": "CORRECTED 2026-09-04 by the first live run. The probe asserted Msg 40510, the Azure statement filter, and the engine says Msg 2812, \"Could not find stored procedure 'sp_configure'.\" Measured on build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure'. The two sidecars in this repository had already contradicted each other on this: azuresql-db-container probe 5 asserted 2812 for EXEC sp_configure and was right, and this one asserted 40510 and was wrong. The distinction is the point of the probe rather than a detail to smooth over. sp_configure is not refused by the filter, it is NOT THERE, and an agent that reads a refusal goes hunting for a permission or a role that would let it through when there is nothing to be permitted to call. The migration instruction is unchanged: an inherited instance level tuning step is deleted, not translated. No SKILL.md or reference in this repository shipped 40510 for sp_configure, so nothing published was wrong; this sidecar's own correction prose said so and has been corrected with it."
      },
      {
        "sql": "ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET RECOVERY SIMPLE;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40517
        },
        "why": "Msg 40517 is the other refusal shape and the one that surprises people: ALTER DATABASE SET is a supported statement, and only this option of it is refused, so a script can be half accepted. Recovery models are on Microsoft Learn's list of syntax not supported in Azure SQL Database, under high availability and database recovery. SETTLED 2026-09-04. This probe was written as a stated hypothesis: 40517 was the documented number for a refused statement option, and no source consulted pinned it to this exact statement, so the why said to correct the number rather than delete the probe if the run disagreed. The run did not disagree. The first live run against build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', answered Msg 40517. The number is now measured rather than reasoned, and no correction was needed."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @cross_database_refused int = 0;\nBEGIN TRY\n  EXEC(N'SELECT TOP (1) name FROM master.sys.objects;');\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n  SET @cross_database_refused = 1;\nEND CATCH\nSELECT @cross_database_refused AS cross_database_refused;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The single database model, made executable. A SQL Server project that keeps lookup tables in one database and application tables in another migrates by merging them, not by rewriting the joins, and this is the probe that shows the three-part name is not merely discouraged. Refusal is asserted rather than a number because neither this skill's text nor its feature matrix states one, and Microsoft Learn documents the restriction without pinning a number to this statement."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_fss_seed (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED, title nvarchar(200) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_fss_seed (title) OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES (N'seeded after provisioning');",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The positive control for the whole migration: once the database is provisioned and the connection selects it, ordinary DDL and DML from the SQL Server project run unchanged. Without a probe like this the sidecar reads as a list of things that break, and the skill's actual promise is that everything else carries over."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "sh -c '! test -e /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d'",
        "where": "container",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The auto-init folder is a PostgreSQL and MySQL convention that this image does not honour, and it is the failure in this skill that produces no error at all: the container starts, the application connects, and the tables are empty. Asserting that the path does not exist in the image is the cheapest available evidence for that, and it is honest about what it measures: the absence of the directory, not the absence of an entrypoint hook. It runs in the container because the claim is about the image."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "/opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd -?",
        "where": "container",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Every rewritten command in this skill hardcodes this absolute path inside the image, including the readiness loop the whole migration depends on. A SQL Server setup that called sqlcmd from PATH will not have noticed the path; if a future image moves it, every code block here breaks and this probe is what says so. It runs in the container, not on the host, because the path is the image's."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-from-sql-server skill"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "I am using mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server in my docker-compose"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "provision an Azure SQL Database in my subscription",
      "connect to SQL Server on my company network"
    ]
  }
}
